---
title: Coleslaw
description: Lesson 7.3. Slicing thin and even, cutting matchsticks, and salting out water.
servings: 4
time: 1 hour 20 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, mastering-the-knife]
lesson: "7.3"
technique: slicing and julienne
status: draft
---

> Technique: slicing. The aim is slices of the same thickness, one
> after another. That depends on the food sitting still, so the first
> cut on anything round is the one that gives it a flat side to rest
> on.

Cut @white cabbage{500%g}(about half a small head) into quarters through the core and cut the core out of each. Lay a quarter flat side down and slice across it as thinly as you can, moving your claw hand back a little after each cut.

> Why flat side down: a stable piece of food does not roll under the
> knife. For anything without a flat side, cut it in half or take a
> thin slice off the bottom first.
-- source: Liz Bomze, "A Guide to Using Your Chef's Knife", America's Test Kitchen, 2018-12-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1362-a-guide-to-using-your-chef-s-knife

Peel @carrot{1}(large) and cut it into 5 cm lengths. Take a thin slice off one side of each length so it sits flat. Cut it into planks 3 mm thick, stack two or three planks, and cut them lengthways into matchsticks 3 mm wide.

> Why even pieces: pieces of the same size behave the same way. In a
> pan they cook at the same rate; here they wilt and take up dressing
> at the same rate.
-- source: Keith Dresser, "How to Cut Carrots", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-06-07, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3368-how-to-cut-carrots (planks, then sticks; 1/8-inch sticks are julienne) ; Liz Bomze, "A Guide to Using Your Chef's Knife", America's Test Kitchen, 2018-12-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1362-a-guide-to-using-your-chef-s-knife ("even-size pieces that cook at the same rate")

Toss the cabbage and carrot with @salt{1%tsp} in a #colander{} set over a bowl. Leave for ~{1%hour}, until wilted. Pat dry with a clean tea towel.

> Why salt first: cabbage is full of water, and in a dressed salad that
> water leaks out and thins the dressing. Salting draws it out
> beforehand. Thin slices matter here too: salt works better with more
> cut surface to act on.
-- source: Caren White, "Why You Should Salt Watery Vegetables Before Cooking", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-12-08, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4102-why-you-should-salt-watery-vegetables-before-cooking (half to 1 teaspoon of salt per pound of vegetables; pat dry, do not rinse) ; Lawman Johnson, "Creamy Coleslaw", America's Test Kitchen, 2018-10-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/recipes/11262-creamy-coleslaw

> Science: the textbook name for this is osmosis: water moves through a
> cell's membrane towards the side with more dissolved material. Salt
> on the cut surface makes the outside far more concentrated than the
> inside of the cells, so water leaves them. You can watch it: liquid
> beads on the cut surfaces within minutes. The test kitchen's pages
> describe the effect and do not name the mechanism. Look up: osmosis,
> hypertonic solution, turgor pressure.
-- source: OpenStax, Biology 2e, section 5.2 "Passive Transport", https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport ; Caren White, "Why You Should Salt Watery Vegetables Before Cooking", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-12-08, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4102-why-you-should-salt-watery-vegetables-before-cooking ("after 5 to 10 minutes, you will notice liquid pooling")

In a #large bowl{}, stir together @mayonnaise{3%tbsp}, @white wine vinegar{1%tbsp}, @sugar{1%tsp} and @black pepper{}. Add the cabbage and carrot and toss. Taste before adding any more salt.

> Next time, without the recipe: give the vegetable a flat side, slice
> it thin and even, salt it, wait, dry it, then dress it.
